      <!-- 3. HOW INFLATION AFFECTS YOUR RETIREMENT CORPUS -->
      <h2>How Inflation Affects Your Retirement Corpus</h2>
      <p>
        Inflation gradually reduces the purchasing power of money over time. A monthly grocery, utility, and healthcare budget of ₹50,000 today will require substantially more rupees to maintain the exact same standard of living decades later.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using our <strong>retirement corpus calculator with inflation</strong> ensures your long-term financial target accounts for compound price increases:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Compounding Effect:</strong> Over a 25-year accumulation timeframe at 6% annual inflation, prices multiply by approximately <strong>4.29 times</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Post-Retirement Inflation:</strong> Inflation does not stop at retirement. Even after age 60, continuing inflation at 6% means your monthly expenses will double again between ages 60 and 72.</li>
        <li><strong>Real Rate of Return:</strong> To preserve your purchasing power, your retirement portfolio must earn a post-retirement return higher than the prevailing rate of inflation.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 4. RETIREMENT CORPUS CALCULATOR INDIA -->
      <h2>Retirement Corpus Calculator India</h2>
      <p>
        When planning for retirement in India, individuals must account for a dynamic economic environment and changing social structures. Using a dedicated <strong>retirement corpus calculator india</strong> helps accommodate specific Indian cost heads such as:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Healthcare & Medical Insurance:</strong> Medical inflation in India often outpaces general headline CPI inflation, making adequate healthcare reserves critical.</li>
        <li><strong>Housing & Utilities:</strong> Factoring in home maintenance, property taxes, electricity, and society charges.</li>
        <li><strong>Lifestyle & Travel:</strong> Post-retirement leisure, pilgrimage, and hobbies.</li>
        <li><strong>Family & Social Commitments:</strong> Occasional support for children, grandchildren, and family functions.</li>
        <li><strong>Absence of Universal Social Security:</strong> Unlike countries with broad government safety nets, Indian retirees primarily rely on their accumulated self-funded corpus across EPF, PPF, NPS, equity mutual funds, and annuities.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 5. HOW MUCH RETIREMENT CORPUS DO YOU NEED? -->
      <h2>How Much Retirement Corpus Do You Need?</h2>
      <p>
        There is no single universal retirement corpus figure that fits every individual. Your required retirement nest egg depends on several personalized parameters:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Current Living Standard & Spending:</strong> Higher current expenditures translate into a larger future corpus requirement.</li>
        <li><strong>Time Horizon to Retirement:</strong> The earlier you start investing, the more power compound interest provides, reducing your required monthly savings.</li>
        <li><strong>Retirement Span:</strong> Retiring at age 50 requires funding 35+ years of expenses, whereas retiring at age 65 requires funding 20 years.</li>
        <li><strong>Asset Allocation & Expected Returns:</strong> A balanced portfolio containing equities and fixed income provides higher expected returns to combat long-term inflation.</li>
        <li><strong>Existing Debt:</strong> Striving to enter retirement debt-free reduces monthly fixed cash flow obligations.</li>
      </ul>

