      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Separate Pre- and Post-Retirement Returns:</strong> Recognizing that your portfolio risk appetite will likely decrease after retirement.</li>
        <li><strong>Annual SIP Step-Up Capability:</strong> Modeling realistic career salary growth where investments increase annually.</li>
        <li><strong>Post-Retirement Drawdown Simulation:</strong> Verifying whether your accumulated corpus lasts across your full life expectancy without early depletion.</li>
        <li><strong>Accounting for Other Income:</strong> Factoring in pensions, annuities, or rental income to avoid overestimating required capital.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- 8. COMMON MISTAKES -->
      <h2>Common Mistakes When Estimating a Retirement Corpus</h2>

      <h3>Ignoring inflation</h3>
      <p>
        Calculating retirement needs using today's expenses without factoring in compounding inflation results in a severe deficit in future decades.
      </p>

      <h3>Underestimating retirement duration</h3>
      <p>
        With advancements in modern healthcare, retirees often live well into their 80s and 90s. Planning for only 15 years of retirement risks running out of money.
      </p>

      <h3>Assuming fixed investment returns</h3>
      <p>
        Markets fluctuate in cycles. Relying on unrealistically high fixed return assumptions throughout both working and retirement years can derail planning.
      </p>

      <h3>Ignoring healthcare and unexpected expenses</h3>
      <p>
        Medical care represents a significant share of expenditure in retirement. Maintaining separate health insurance and emergency reserves is vital.
      </p>

      <h3>Not increasing savings over time</h3>
      <p>
        Keeping monthly SIP contributions constant over a 25-year career ignores income growth. Stepping up investments by 5% to 10% annually dramatically boosts the final nest egg.
      </p>

      <h3>Treating the calculation as a guarantee</h3>
      <p>
        Retirement estimates should be treated as dynamic blueprints. Regularly reviewing your progress every 1 to 2 years ensures you adapt to lifestyle and market shifts.
      </p>

